title: Repository
tags: Daemon
kind: overview
aliases: repo repositories
summary: A directory tree and its metadata store in .metafolder/ — one store per repository, loaded by one daemon at a time.

A ''repository'' is a directory tree — its ''root'' — together with the
metadata metafolder keeps about it, in a `.metafolder/` directory. Every
metarecord ([[Metarecord]]) belongs to the repository whose store holds it: there is one store per
repository, and a metarecord cannot belong to two. Links between repositories are
the business of sync, outside the data model.

`.metafolder/` usually sits inside the root, but can live anywhere — an
''external'' `.metafolder`, for a read-only data disk or to keep the metadata on a
faster device. Its `internal/` part (the store and the daemon's own files) is
never tracked; the rest (`config.json`, `schema.json`, the GUI's input histories)
is ordinary content you may track like any file
([[Repository layout on disk]]).

A daemon ''loads'' a repository to work on it, and holds its store under an
exclusive lock while it does: a second daemon cannot load the same repository.
Names are unique among the loaded repositories, so a name designates one
([[Creating and loading a repository]]).
